id: L-2026-10-18-builder-162751-001
type: insight
timestamp: '2026-10-18T16:27:51.908Z'
author: builder
title: 'Decision: the GUI re-pins a changed fragment key visibly, never silently'
summary: A launch-fragment kernel key that differs from the page's localStorage pin is trusted (it came through the authenticated gnomon open channel) and re-pinned, with a sticky kernel-owned notice naming old/new keyIds, cleared only by a user click, plus a /diag line. Reconnect (no fragment) verifies against the pin; no pin and no fragment refuses.
symbols_touched:
  - '#gui'
  - '#web-endpoint'
files_modified:
  - packages/gnomon-gui/src/core/launch.ts
  - packages/gnomon-gui/src/ui/app.ts
  - packages/gnomon-gui/static/index.html
  - docs/specs/gnomon.md
commit: 673456ae
learnings:
  - Refusing on a fragment/pin mismatch would loop forever after a key rotation because gnomon open hands out the same new key
  - The visible trail (sticky notice + gui.log line) is what makes trusting the fragment safe
tags:
  - decision
  - gnomon
  - m1-10
  - aegis-c2
  - s25-m4
body: Spec §4.9 said a mismatch refuses; that reading cannot survive a legitimate kernel key rotation. Aegis C2 conditioned approval on a sticky, kernel-owned, keyId-naming, logged re-pin; spec §4.9 (S25-M4) and §11.2 rule 2 amended in 673456ae. Recorded as an insight because lore type decision was removed in v6 and the decision CLI is MCP-only here.
